      <node id="113992328163224810" label="@mappingsupport@m.ai6yr.org: @exador23 @ai6yr One thing that was learned from the 2018 Montecito #…">
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="0" value="110433002329275149" />
          <attvalue for="1" value="mappingsupport@m.ai6yr.org" />
          <attvalue for="2" value="2025-02-12T18:28:06.000Z" />
          <attvalue for="3" value="en" />
          <attvalue for="4" value="@exador23 @ai6yr One thing that was learned from the 2018 Montecito #DebrisFlow was that at bends in the channel the material sometimes tends to pile up and then overshoot the bank on the downward side. The debris flow then continues *outside* the channel by finding a new path of least resistance which could very well be right down streets, through yards, etc." />
          <attvalue for="5" value="debrisflow" />
          <attvalue for="6" value="context_descendants" />
          <attvalue for="7" value="1" />
          <attvalue for="8" value="2" />
          <attvalue for="9" value="4" />
          <attvalue for="10" value="1" />
          <attvalue for="11" value="0" />
          <attvalue for="12" value="2" />
          <attvalue for="13" value="1" />
          <attvalue for="14" value="1" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="113992458057999983" label="@exador23@m.ai6yr.org: @mappingsupport @ai6yr Anywhere the flow would slow or go through a b…">
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="0" value="109366550461584397" />
          <attvalue for="1" value="exador23@m.ai6yr.org" />
          <attvalue for="2" value="2025-02-12T19:01:10.000Z" />
          <attvalue for="3" value="en" />
          <attvalue for="4" value="@mappingsupport @ai6yr Anywhere the flow would slow or go through a bottleneck too. Allows debris to settle out which then fills up the space and as additional material comes down it spills out in unexpected directions. Learned a LOT about flows from this lecture a couple weeks ago by caltech geologist Michael Lamb https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N5M9LlZhAIo first 12 minutes is just introduction stuff. The new (to me at least) science starts around the 30 minute point. Turns out the hydrophobic…" />
          <attvalue for="5" value="" />
          <attvalue for="6" value="context_descendants" />
          <attvalue for="7" value="0" />
          <attvalue for="8" value="8" />
          <attvalue for="9" value="9" />
          <attvalue for="10" value="1" />
          <attvalue for="11" value="0" />
          <attvalue for="12" value="1" />
          <attvalue for="13" value="1" />
          <attvalue for="14" value="0" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
